        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Yamnaya culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-bug">Southern Bug</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> and Ural rivers, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; its core in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>–Don steppe; the largest fortified site at Mikhailovka</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 3300–2600 BCE (also given as 3300–2500 BCE); core Yamnaya c. 3750–3350 BCE; early horizon spread c. 3400–3200 BCE; followed by the Catacomb (2800–2200 BCE) and Poltavka (2700–2100 BCE) cultures</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">pit graves under kurgans, with bodies covered in ochre; two-wheeled carts and four-wheeled wagons; nomadic or semi-nomadic herding; carved stone stelae; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> hypothesis of Proto-Indo-European origins; ‘steppe ancestry’</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated kurgan burials since Vasily Gorodtsov’s work of 1901–1903, grave goods and wagons, stable isotopes, and ancient DNA studies from 2015 on; no writing</span></div>
