    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Yamnaya culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe.jpg" alt="Pontic–Caspian steppe" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Pontic–Caspian steppe <span class="kid-tale-cr">YegorGeologist, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 5,000 years ago, the Yamnaya lived on the steppe of today’s Ukraine and southern Russia.</p><p>Their name comes from a Russian word about pits, after their pit graves.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>They were herders who moved their animals across the steppe.</p><p>They travelled in carts and wagons, probably pulled by oxen, and perhaps rode horses too.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copper.jpg" alt="Copper" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Copper <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jonathan Zander; derivative work by Materialscientist, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Chiefs led their groups, and metalworkers were held in special honour.</p><p>They probably ate meat, milk, yogurt and cheese.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kurgan.jpg" alt="Kurgan" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Kurgan <span class="kid-tale-cr">Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Some of the dead were laid under earth mounds called kurgans, covered in ochre, a coloured earth.</p><p>Some important people were buried with whole wooden wagons.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages.jpg" alt="Indo-European languages" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Indo-European languages <span class="kid-tale-cr">Bill Williams, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Many scholars think they spoke an early form of the Indo-European languages.</p><p>DNA links them to many later people of Europe and South Asia, though scholars debate how far.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>‘Yamnaya’ is an archaeologists’ label from a Russian word meaning ‘related to pits’, after the pit graves under burial mounds that mark this herding culture of the steppe.</li><li>Its people moved their herds across the steppe in carts and wagons and were organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>; whether they also rode horses is disputed.</li><li>The widely accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> hypothesis holds that they spoke a stage of Proto-Indo-European, and DNA links them to much later European and South Asian ancestry, though critics question how far.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In some areas elite members of the Yamnaya culture were buried with complete wooden wagons.</p></aside>
    </section>

